    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193511": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Natural Resource Policy, Department of Society and Conservation, W.A. Franke College of Forestry and Conservation",
      "description": "Department: University of Montana: Provost: Missoula: W. A. Franke College of Forestry and Conservation Location: Missoula, MT, United States The Department of Society and Conservation in the W.A. Franke College of Forestry and Conservation at the University of Montana invites applications for a full-time academic year tenure-track Assistant Professor of Natural Resource Policy to join our interdisciplinary social science faculty. We are seeking candidates who have the ability to apply policy to a range of contemporary real-world challenges in natural resource management and environmental conservation. This position will be part of the Environmental Science and Sustainability Program, an interdisciplinary undergraduate program that connects science and solutions to provide students with the knowledge and skills to solve complex environmental problems and advance sustainability. The successful candidate will teach policy courses serving students across degree programs within the Franke College. Courses may include undergraduate Natural Resources Policy, as well as offerings in Water Policy, Climate Policy, Wildlife Policy, and/or Environmental Politics and Policy, depending on the College’s needs and the successful candidate’s expertise. The successful candidate will also mentor and supervise undergraduate and graduate students (MS and PhD); lead an impactful, externally-funded research program; and participate in service activities for the Program, Department, College, and University. We welcome candidates who value “partnering with place,” a UM Priority for Action focused on engagement with Montana communities and landscapes, and the application of research to practice at the federal, state, and tribal levels. For detailed information regarding faculty duties and expectations, please contact the Search Chair, Laurie Yung , to request a copy of the Dept. of Society and Conservation Unit Standards. Position Details Title: Assistant Professor of Natural Resource Policy Schedule: Full-time academic year position beginning fall semester 2027 Salary: $70,000-$85,000 per year commensurate with qualifications Benefits: Medical Insurance/Mandatory Retirement Contribution/Professional Development/Partial Tuition Waiver/Wellness About Us The Department of Society and Conservation is an interdisciplinary social science department within the W.A. Franke College of Forestry and Conservation at The University of Montana. We are dedicated to research and education that addresses our most pressing human-environment challenges. We value multiple approaches to pedagogy and to social science inquiry, and we embrace interdisciplinary collaboration in and out of the classroom. Our faculty have expertise in human dimensions of natural resources, environmental governance and public lands policy, outdoor recreation and nature-based tourism, sustainable communities and livelihoods, wildland and protected area management, international conservation and development, and political ecology and environmental justice. We work at local to global scales, connecting science and practice to engage a range of decision-makers and communities. The Franke College is highly interdisciplinary and promotes collaboration across disciplines and departments in both teaching and research. Additional information can be found at https://www.umt.edu/environment/ . Missoula is a wonderful place to live and work and is home to a wide range of renowned writers, artists, musicians, and outdoor enthusiasts. Required Qualifications PhD (earned doctorate) or JD with a focus on environmental or natural resource policy or governance, or a related social science or interdisciplinary field (Degree must be conferred by August 2027). Demonstrated potential for effective teaching. Passion for and ability to teach natural resource or environmental policy courses. Record of scholarly activity. Demonstrated potential for a nationally-recognized, externally-funded research program. Demonstrated potential for applied or policy-engaged research with private, public, tribal, and non-profit partners. Preferred Qualifications Expertise in natural resource policy in the U.S. West. Demonstrated excellence in teaching and mentoring undergraduate students. Experience teaching natural resource or environmental policy. Experience working with policy-making or policy makers in the non-profit, government, or another relevant sector. Demonstrated success in building and sustaining partnerships across private, public, tribal, and non-profit organizations. Interest in and demonstrated potential for engaging with Montana communities and landscapes. Record of securing external funding to support scholarly activity. Peer, national, or international recognition in candidate’s field. Screening of applications will begin after the closing date of October 25, 2026 , however, applications will continue to be accepted until an adequate applicant pool has been established. Complete applications received by the closing date will be guaranteed consideration. To apply, please upload the following materials: Cover letter (2 page max.; 11pt font; 1” margins) describing your interest in the position and relevant experience in both teaching and research Detailed CV listing education and describing work experience Three (3) professional references - Names and contact information One recent, relevant publication or other scholarly output. Teaching statement (1 page max.; 11pt font; 1” margins) addressing (1) how you would teach an undergraduate Natural Resource Policy course to students in the Environmental Science and Sustainability, Forestry, Wildlife Biology, and Parks, Tourism, and Recreation Management majors, and (2) why you want to teach policy courses. Please contact Search Committee Chair Laurie Yung (laurie.yung@umontana.edu) with any questions. The University of Montana is interested in receiving applications from people who would assist the University in demonstrating its five priorities for action : Place student success at the center of all we do; drive excellence and innovation in teaching, learning, and research; embody the principle of “mission first, people always\"; partner with place; and proudly tell the UM story."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/193529": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Silviculture, Department of Forest Management, W.A. Franke College of Forestry and Conservation",
      "description": "Department: University of Montana: Provost: Missoula: W. A. Franke College of Forestry and Conservation Location: Missoula, MT, United States The Department of Forest Management (DFM) in the W.A. Franke College of Forestry and Conservation at the University of Montana invites applications for a full-time academic year tenure-track Assistant Professor of Silviculture . The candidate will advance the DFM’s mission to provide innovation and leadership in the use of active forest management to achieve beneficial ecological and socioeconomic outcomes. This mission includes educating future forestry professionals, engaging in cutting-edge research to solve real-world problems, and contributing outward facing service to the discipline of forestry. The DFM welcomes applications from individuals with diverse perspectives and backgrounds. We encourage applications from veterans, women, persons of color, indigenous peoples, persons with disabilities and other individuals who can contribute to our intellectual diversity and cultural richness. The successful candidate will develop an extramurally funded research program and demonstrate a commitment to translating their research into applications relevant to contemporary forest management. The College’s Lubrecht Experimental Forest offers a distinctive opportunity for field-based silvicultural research, teaching, and demonstration, and candidates are encouraged to consider how this resource could support the growth of their program. Teaching responsibilities include contributions to the Society of American Foresters accredited professional B.S. Forestry curriculum. Specific courses to be taught include undergraduate and graduate courses in silviculture as well as other courses as assigned. The successful candidate will mentor graduate students at the MS and PhD level and contribute to undergraduate advising and mentoring. The successful candidate will engage in relevant service within the forestry discipline, as well as service to the department, college, and university which is expected to be scaled upon establishment of productive research and teaching programs. For detailed information regarding faculty duties and expectations, please contact the Search Chair, Andrew Larson a.larson@umontana.edu, to request a copy of the Dept. of Forest Management Unit Standards. Position Details Title: Assistant Professor of Silviculture Schedule: Full-time academic year position beginning fall semester 2027 Salary: $70,000-$85,000 per year commensurate with qualifications Benefits: Medical Insurance/Mandatory Retirement Contribution/Professional Development/Partial Tuition Waiver/Wellness About Us The Department of Forest Management comprises a multidisciplinary faculty focused on developing the foundation and tools necessary for sustainable management of forest landscapes. DFM occupies a unique position in FCFC as steward of a nationally accredited professional degree in Forestry that has served students since 1913. DFM members are active in basic and applied research with noteworthy productivity in fire and water sciences, remote sensing, biometrics, forest and fire ecology, and silviculture. DFM faculty direct the Montana Climate Office, the National Center for Landscape Fire Analysis, and the Wilderness Institute, each with significant outlays in infrastructure, grants and contracts, staff, and graduate students. DFM’s teaching, research, and outreach are enhanced by Lubrecht Experimental Forest and Bandy Ranch, both located within easy driving distance of campus in the Blackfoot Valley. FCFC is highly interdisciplinary and has multiple programs that promote collaboration across departments in both teaching and research. Additional information about the college can be found at https://www.umt.edu/environment/ . Required Qualifications: PhD (earned doctorate) in forestry, natural resource management, forest science, or a related field by the date of hire. Record of scholarly publications in peer-reviewed outlets that indicates potential to achieve national distinction in silviculture, applied forest ecology, and/or forest management. Potential to develop a nationally recognized and extramurally funded research program that will inform forest management in the western U.S. and beyond. Demonstrated potential for excellence and innovation in undergraduate and graduate forestry education. Record of effective collaboration, grounded in ethics, openness, collegiality, and mutual respect. Preferred Qualifications: DFM encourages applications from individuals meeting the required qualifications, and is particularly interested in candidates with one or more of the following: Demonstrated track record of acquiring external research funding. Experience with silvicultural field experiments and long-term studies. Experience teaching and mentoring undergraduate students. Experience using high-impact learning practices, including field learning, service-learning, internships, and other types of experiential learning. Professional forestry certification such as Certified Forester (CF), Registered Professional Forester (RPF), or equivalent. Record of translating research results into real-world forest management applications. Screening of applications will begin after the closing date of October 25, 2026 ; however, applications will continue to be accepted until an adequate applicant pool has been established. Complete applications received by the closing date will be guaranteed consideration. To apply, please upload the following materials: Cover letter (3 page maximum). Please describe your relevant experience and explain how you meet the required and preferred qualifications; characterize your teaching philosophy and interests; share your vision for building a research program; and tell us what excites you about joining the University of Montana and working with its diverse student body and faculty. Detailed curriculum vitae (CV). Names and contact information for three (3) professional references . The University of Montana is interested in receiving applications from people who would assist the University in demonstrating its five priorities for action : Place student success at the center of all we do; drive excellence and innovation in teaching, learning, and research; embody the principle of “mission first, people always\"; partner with place; and proudly tell the UM story."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195106": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate, Immunology and Bioinformatics, Center for Translation Medicine",
      "description": "Department: University of Montana: Provost: Missoula: Center for Translational Medicine Location: Missoula, MT, United States The joint immunology lab of Drs. Blair DeBuysscher and Jay Evans at the University of Montana is seeking a Post Doctoral Scientist to join our pioneering research in developing novel vaccines. Join our immunology team and play a pivotal role in the advancement of next-generation vaccines. We're looking for driven individuals passionate about understanding the mechanism of action around innovative adjuvants and vaccines. Ideally, candidates will possess a robust understanding of multiomic approaches and bioinformatics. Proficiency in immunology, flow cytometry, and mouse handling would be highly advantageous. Our multidisciplinary team is dedicated to exploring innovative strategies at the convergence of immunology, formulation science and chemistry, to tackle challenges in vaccine design and development. We are a well-funded, collaborative research team with strong connections in academia and industry. Our team is at the forefront of vaccine research, focusing on a range of diseases impacting human health, including tuberculosis, COVID-19, monkeypox, Lyme disease, Flu, RSV, and opioid addiction. Additionally, we delve into understanding host-pathogen interactions, immunopathology, and immune-mediated protection at the molecular level. Apply and become a part of our team of creative and forward-thinking scientists in beautiful Montana. Position details Position is full-time, 1.0 FTE, Letter of Appointment and includes a comprehensive and competitive benefits package including insurance, mandatory retirement plan, partial tuition waiver, and wellness program. Salary for this position is consistent with NIH pay scales, starting $61,008. Minimum Qualifications: Ph.D., M.D., D.V.M. (current or within 3 months for graduate students). Strong working knowledge of immunology, bioinformatics and/or molecular biology. Extensive experience with statistical analysis (R or Python). Ability to work independently and collaboratively within a larger team framework to design, execute, and interpret experiments crucial to the development of novel vaccines and immunotherapeutic. Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Proficiency in accurate record-keeping methodologies. Eagerness to learn and apply new skills appropriate to conduct necessary research. Preferred Qualifications: Previous experience with one or more of the following techniques is preferred; cell culture, cell signaling, receptor/ligand interaction, molecular biology, animal handling and procedures, flow cytometry or fluorescence microscopy. Strong publication record. Screening of applications will begin after the closing date of October 21, 2026 ; however, applications will continue to be accepted until an adequate applicant pool has been established. Complete applications received by the closing date will be guaranteed consideration. To apply, please upload the following materials: Letter of Interest – addressing the stated required skills for the position Detailed CV listing education and describing work experience Three (3) professional references - Names and contact information The University of Montana is interested in receiving applications from people who would assist the University in demonstrating its five priorities for action : Place student success at the center of all we do; drive excellence and innovation in teaching, learning, and research; embody the principle of “mission first, people always\"; partner with place; and proudly tell the UM story."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195814": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor, School of Speech, Language, Hearing, and Occupational Sciences",
      "description": "Department: University of Montana: Provost: Missoula: College of Health: Speech, Language, Hearing, and Occupational Sciences Location: Missoula, MT, United States The University of Montana, School of Speech, Language, Hearing, and Occupational Sciences (SLHOS), within the College of Health, invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant or Associate Professor to begin August 2027. Responsibilities: Build a robust research program and pursue external research funding Teach graduate and undergraduate courses (academic and/or clinical) through campus-based, synchronous online, or asynchronous online instruction, depending on the academic program Mentor students and direct their research Foster interprofessional research and training collaborations with international, regional, and campus partners Contribute to school, college, and university, service About the College of Health and School of Speech, Language, Hearing, and Occupational Sciences College of Health School of SLHOS About the University of Montana UM is the fastest growing flagship university in Montana with over 11,000 students and is a national leader in access, especially for first-generation and low-income students. Our mission is inclusive prosperity and to transform lives by providing a high-quality and accessible education and by generating world-class research and creative scholarship in an exceptional place. We integrate the liberal arts and sciences into undergraduate, graduate and professional studies to shape global citizens who are creative and agile learners committed to expanding the boundaries of knowledge and to building and sustaining diverse communities. The University of Montana is interested in receiving applications from people who would assist the University in demonstrating its five priorities for action : Place student success at the center of all we do; drive excellence and innovation in teaching, learning, and research; embody the principle of “mission first, people always\"; partner with place; and proudly tell the UM story. About Missoula and Montana Destination Missoula Visit Montana Position Details Tenurable Faculty: The School of Speech, Language, Hearing, and Occupational Sciences Title: Assistant or Associate Professor (rank is determined by qualifications and experience) Schedule: Full time, academic year position Salary: $80,000- $95,000 (salary is determined by qualifications and experience) Benefits: Medical Insurance, Mandatory Retirement Contribution, Professional Development, Partial Tuition Waiver, Wellness Program Primary Duties: Primary duties include teaching, scholarship, and service. Teaching responsibilities may include a combination of required, elective, and clinical courses A research doctoral degree (or ABD) in speech, language, and hearing sciences, communication disorders and sciences, or related field Specialty in any area of speech-language pathology, audiology, and other communication disorders Record of scholarship and professional or clinical service sufficient to merit appointment at the Associate or Assistant Professor level Capable of acquiring extramural funding to support documented research agendas Ability to teach independently in a graduate clinical program Excellent oral, written, and interpersonal skills including the ability to work with a variety of populations Ability to learn and apply new technologies Preferred Qualifications: Expertise in any of the following areas: complex communication disorders, augmentative and alternative communication, early language development, early intervention, medical pediatrics, child feeding and swallowing, craniofacial disorders, pediatric/adult motor speech, traumatic brain injury, fluency disorders, autism spectrum disorder, audiology, aural rehabilitation Clinical certification in Speech-Language Pathology or Audiology Eligibility for Montana State licensure. Evidence of successful academic, funding, or administrative experience at the University level Established record of obtaining extramural funding for research Priority Application Date: November 15, 2026 09:59pm mountain time Complete applications received by this date will be guaranteed consideration. Required Application Materials: Letter of Interest addressing your qualifications and experience in research, teaching, and service Detailed Curriculum Vitae (CV) Three (3) professional references: names and contact information"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196485": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Anthropology",
      "description": "Department: University of Montana: Provost: Missoula: College of Humanities and Social Sciences: Anthropology Location: Missoula, MT, United States Posted: Oct 27, 2026 Deadline: Nov 29, 2026 The University of Montana, Department of Anthropology, within the College of Humanities and Social Sciences, invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor to begin August, 2027. The specific sub-discipline within Biological Anthropology is open, particular preference will be given to those with expertise in paleopathology, dentition, and/or advanced computational statistics. Responsibilities: Support framework of a four-field approach, utilized by the department Teach a range of courses, such as introductory, upper-division, and graduate seminars Include forensic casework as part of research agenda Serve on topically related graduate committees, and mentor students in forensic and bioarcheology subjects Bring on graduate students at the MA and PhD level to support Anthropology’s robust graduate program About the College of Humanities and Social Sciences and Anthropology College of Humanities and Social Sciences Anthropology About the University of Montana UM is the fastest growing flagship university in Montana with over 11,000 students and is a national leader in access, especially for first-generation and low-income students. Our mission is inclusive prosperity and to transform lives by providing a high-quality and accessible education and by generating world-class research and creative scholarship in an exceptional place. We integrate the liberal arts and sciences into undergraduate, graduate, and professional studies to shape global citizens who are creative and agile learners committed to expanding the boundaries of knowledge and to building and sustaining diverse communities. The University of Montana is interested in receiving applications from people who would assist the University in demonstrating its five priorities for action : Place student success at the center of all we do; drive excellence and innovation in teaching, learning, and research; embody the principle of “mission first, people always\"; partner with place; and proudly tell the UM story. About Missoula and Montana Destination Missoula Visit Montana Position Details Tenurable Faculty: Department of Anthropology Title: Assistant Professor Schedule: Full time, academic year Salary: $70,000- $85,000 (salary is dependent on qualifications and experience) Benefits: Medical Insurance, Mandatory Retirement Contribution, Professional Development, Partial Tuition Waiver, Wellness Program Primary Duties: Primary duties include teaching, scholarship, and service. Teaching responsibilities may include a combination of required and elective courses. A Ph.D. (conferred before August 2027) with an emphasis and an adviser in Biological Anthropology Demonstrated evidence of successful teaching experience at the undergraduate and graduate level A successful research agenda in forensic anthropology and/or bioarcheology as evidenced by funding, publications, presentations, and experience Experience with forensic anthropological casework that demonstrates an ability to apply osteological and forensic skills Commitment to working with graduate students on research projects to contribute to the field of bioarcheology and forensic anthropology Commitment to supporting the mission of the College of Humanities and Social Sciences through collegiality, interdisciplinarity, and student service Preferred Qualifications: Area of research focus that compliment but does not overlap significantly with the overall department portfolio Demonstrated ability to apply, receive, and manage grant funding Ability to network with surrounding Tribal entities to aid in Missing and Murdered Indigenous Peoples (MMIP) case work Priority Application Date: November 29, 2026 09:59pm mountain time Complete applications received by this date will be guaranteed consideration. Required Application Materials: Letter of Interest addressing your qualifications and experience (two pages) Detailed Curriculum Vitae (CV) Three (3) professional references: names and contact information Teaching Statement and other evidence of teaching abilities"
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/195428": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor, Chemistry and Biochemistry",
      "description": "Department: University of Montana: Provost: Missoula: College of Science: Chemistry and Biochemistry Location: Missoula, MT, United States Posted: Oct 16, 2026 Deadline: Nov 15, 2026 The University of Montana, Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry, within the College of Science, invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor in Analytical/Environmental Chemistry to begin August 2027. The department is seeking an innovative candidate whose research complements the University's strengths in environmental sciences, including chemical ecology, aquatic chemistry, atmospheric chemistry, and biogeochemistry. A focus on developing and applying analytical methods to address environmental problems is essential, though theoretical approaches, including modeling and AI/MI-driven approaches, will also be considered. The Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry is home to twelve tenured faculty members and more than twenty PhD students, with annual sponsored research expenditures exceeding five million dollars. The Analytical/Environmental Chemistry branch is well connected to a broad network of research centers and facilities at the University and throughout the region, including the Center for Biomolecular Structure and Dynamics, the Division of Biological and Biomedical Sciences, the Flathead Lake Biological Station, the Center for Population Health Research, the W. A. Franke College of Forestry and Conservation, the National Center for Landscape Fire Analysis, and the US Forest Service Missoula Fire Sciences Laboratory. These connections provide exceptional opportunities for interdisciplinary research spanning chemistry, ecology, environmental science, public health, and natural resource science. The successful candidate will be expected to build on these existing strengths while developing new collaborations and research directions that further expand the University's capabilities in analytical and environmental chemistry. Responsibilities: Lead and participate in teaching courses such as analytical chemistry and environmental- and ecology-related courses at both undergraduate and graduate levels Build on the University's existing strengths in analytical and environmental chemistry Conduct interdisciplinary research spanning chemistry, ecology, environmental science, public health, and natural resource science Develop new collaborations and research directions Expand the University's research capabilities in analytical and environmental chemistry About the College of Science and Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry College of Science Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry About the University of Montana UM is the fastest growing flagship university in Montana with over 11,000 students and is a national leader in access, especially for first-generation and low-income students. Our mission is inclusive prosperity and to transform lives by providing a high-quality and accessible education and by generating world-class research and creative scholarship in an exceptional place. We integrate the liberal arts and sciences into undergraduate, graduate and professional studies to shape global citizens who are creative and agile learners committed to expanding the boundaries of knowledge and to building and sustaining diverse communities. The University of Montana is interested in receiving applications from people who would assist the University in demonstrating its five priorities for action : Place student success at the center of all we do; drive excellence and innovation in teaching, learning, and research; embody the principle of “mission first, people always\"; partner with place; and proudly tell the UM story. About Missoula and Montana Destination Missoula Visit Montana Position Details Tenurable Faculty: Department of Chemistry and Biochemistry Title: Assistant Professor Schedule: Full time, academic year Salary: $70,000- $85,000 (salary depends on qualifications and experience) Benefits: Medical Insurance, Mandatory Retirement Contribution, Professional Development, Partial Tuition Waiver, Wellness Program Primary Duties: Primary duties include teaching, scholarship, research, and service Ph.D. in chemistry or related field of study A strong record of research and scholarship, including post-doctoral research experience and training, or equivalent A commitment to excellence in teaching and effective mentorship of graduate or undergraduate students from all backgrounds Experience communicating effectively with students, professionals, and the public Evidence of working collegially with students, staff, faculty, and external stakeholders Preferred Qualifications: Experience with developing competitive extramurally funded grants Priority Application Date : November 08, 2026 09:59pm mountain time Complete applications received by this date will be guaranteed consideration. To receive full consideration. Required Application Materials: Letter of Interest addressing your qualifications and experience in both teaching and research (one page) Detailed Curriculum Vitae (CV), and if available, including a summary of recent teaching evaluations Three (3) professional references: names and contact information Teaching Statement describing teaching experiences and pedagogical approaches, areas of teaching expertise, and approaches to engage and mentor students (one page) Research Statement describing plans for a future independent research program that synergizes with UM’s strengths and core facilities (two pages) Nominations Nominations welcomed. Please submit at our nominations page ."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196412": {
      "title": "Instructor of English Education, Department of Teaching and Learning,  Phyllis J. Washington College of Education",
      "description": "Department: University of Montana: Provost: Missoula: Phyllis J. Washington College of Education Location: Montana, United States Posted: Oct 27, 2026 The Department of Teaching and Learning in the Phyllis J. Washington College of Education at the University of Montana invites applications for a full-time, Instructor of English Education .  Employment is offered on an academic year contract, with the possibility of annual reappointment. The successful applicant will contribute to a dynamic, field-based teacher preparation program and a K–12 Literacy Endorsement option grounded in strong partnerships with local schools. The instructor will teach courses in literacy and language arts education, supervise teacher candidates in partner school settings, engage in service for the department, and advise undergraduate and graduate students. We seek a colleague committed to preparing future educators through practice-based, community-engaged approaches to teaching and learning. For more information about specific faculty duties and expectations, please contact the search chair, Dr. Matthew Schertz, matthew.schertz@umontana.edu , to request a copy of the Department of Teaching and Learning unit standards. Position Details Non-Tenure Track: Instructor of English Education Schedule: Full-time academic year position beginning fall semester 2027 Salary: $65,000-$70,000 commensurate with qualifications Benefits: Medical Insurance/Mandatory Retirement Contribution/Professional Development/Partial Tuition Waiver/Wellness Required Qualifications: M.A. in English Education or related field. Experience teaching students in grades 4–12. Record of literacy leadership. Preferred Qualifications: Earned doctorate in English Education, Literacy Studies, or a closely related field. Evidence of an established or emerging record of scholarship. Substantial experience working with students from a variety of backgrounds. Demonstrated evidence of successful work in teacher education. Expertise in English teaching, with strengths in some of the following areas: young adult literature; adolescent literacy; writing pedagogy; intervention; rural education; culturally responsive pedagogy, Indigenous education; and/or digital literacies. Screening of applications will begin after the closing date of November 29, 2026 ; however, applications will continue to be accepted until an adequate applicant pool has been established. Complete applications received by the closing date will be guaranteed consideration. To apply, please upload the following materials: Letter of Interest addressing the stated required skills for the position Detailed CV listing education and describing work experience Three (3) professional references - Names and contact information Transcripts -Unofficial acceptable for application process Teaching Evaluations The University of Montana is interested in receiving applications from people who would assist the University in demonstrating its five priorities for action : Place student success at the center of all we do; drive excellence and innovation in teaching, learning, and research; embody the principle of “mission first, people always\"; partner with place; and proudly tell the UM story."
    },
    "https://apply.interfolio.com/196548": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate, Adjuvant Discovery and Vaccine Development, Center for Translation Medicine",
      "description": "Department: University of Montana: Provost: Missoula: Center for Translational Medicine Location: Missoula, MT, United States Posted: Oct 28, 2026 The Center for Translational Medicine at the University of Montana, Missoula MT, is seeking a postdoctoral research scientist to join the collaborative lab of Drs. Blair DeBuysscher and Jay Evans. Be a part of our interactive and multidisciplinary research team dedicated to pioneering the discovery and development of innovative vaccines. Our focus spans a wide spectrum of diseases affecting human health, including COVID-19, influenza virus, tuberculosis, pertussis, and opioid addiction. Additionally, we study the molecular intricacies of host-pathogen interactions, vaccine-mediated immunity, and their impact on immunopathology and immune-mediated protection. Join our multidisciplinary team and play a pivotal role in the advancement of next-generation adjuvants and vaccines. We're looking for driven individuals passionate about designing and developing innovative adjuvants and vaccines. Ideally, candidates will possess a robust understanding of immunology or molecular biology, along with hands-on experience in techniques crucial for investigating cell-mediated immunity, cell signaling, and receptor/ligand interactions. Proficiency in mouse handling, flow cytometry, and bioinformatics analysis would be highly advantageous. Embark on a new journey and become a part of our team of creative and forward-thinking scientists in beautiful Montana. Nestled amidst stunning landscapes, our research environment offers not only groundbreaking scientific opportunities but also an unparalleled quality of life. Position details Position is full-time, 1.0 FTE, Letter of Appointment and includes a comprehensive and competitive benefits package including insurance, mandatory retirement plan, partial tuition waiver, and wellness program. Salary for this position is consistent with NIH pay scales, starting $61,008. Minimum Qualifications: Ph.D., M.D., D.V.M. (current or within 3 months for graduate students). Strong working knowledge of immunology, bioinformatics and/or molecular biology. Ability to work independently and collaboratively within a larger team framework to design, execute, and interpret experiments crucial to the development of novel vaccines and immunotherapeutic. Excellent written and verbal communication skills. Proficiency in accurate record-keeping methodologies. Eagerness to learn and apply new skills appropriate to conduct necessary research. Preferred Qualifications: Previous experience with one or more of the following techniques is preferred; cell culture, cell signaling, receptor/ligand interaction, molecular biology, animal handling and procedures, flow cytometry or fluorescence microscopy. Strong publication record. Screening of applications will begin after the closing date of November 11, 2026 ; however, applications will continue to be accepted until an adequate applicant pool has been established. Complete applications received by the closing date will be guaranteed consideration. To apply, please upload the following materials: Letter of Interest – addressing the stated required skills for the position Detailed CV listing education and describing work experience Three (3) professional references - Names and contact information The University of Montana is interested in receiving applications from people who would assist the University in demonstrating its five priorities for action : Place student success at the center of all we do; drive excellence and innovation in teaching, learning, and research; embody the principle of “mission first, people always\"; partner with place; and proudly tell the UM story."
    }
  },
